WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.460
An agent is useful when people repeatedly work toward the same goal but must make slightly different decisions each time.

00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:14.967
A task that always has exactly the same steps can often be handled by a simple rule.

00:00:14.967 --> 00:00:19.227
Look for a place the program can get reliable information.

00:00:19.227 --> 00:00:25.117
A question about a specific delivery needs an order record or a carrier update.

00:00:25.117 --> 00:00:29.005
A confident sentence on its own tells you very little.

00:00:29.005 --> 00:00:34.545
Choose a first job where a person can check the result before it affects anybody.

00:00:34.545 --> 00:00:42.233
Drafting a message is easier to undo than sending an incorrect promise to hundreds of customers.

00:00:42.233 --> 00:00:45.978
Imagine a camping shop gets a supplier notice.

00:00:45.978 --> 00:00:49.558
Its green Trailhead tents will arrive two weeks late.

00:00:49.558 --> 00:00:55.351
Some customers are waiting for that tent, but others ordered boots or sleeping mats.

00:00:55.351 --> 00:01:02.179
Sending the same warning to everybody would worry customers who have nothing to do with the delay.

00:01:02.179 --> 00:01:05.866
So how do you choose a good first job for an agent?

00:01:05.866 --> 00:01:12.409
First, it is repetitive: it comes up many times a week, and each time it is a little different.

00:01:12.409 --> 00:01:20.872
Second, it needs lookups: the answer lives in a system, like an orders database, not in the question itself.

00:01:20.872 --> 00:01:28.174
Third, mistakes are recoverable: if the agent gets one wrong, someone can spot it and fix it cheaply.

00:01:28.174 --> 00:01:36.036
A job that fails these checks is usually better done by a person, a help page or a simple workflow.

00:01:36.036 --> 00:01:40.156
The agent opens the list of orders still waiting to ship.

00:01:40.156 --> 00:01:43.320
It keeps only those containing the green tent.

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:48.156
From that shorter list it prepares a message for each affected order.

00:01:48.156 --> 00:01:53.044
A useful draft names the item and reports the supplier’s delay.

00:01:53.044 --> 00:01:57.750
It leaves out a delivery date the supplier never confirmed.

00:01:57.750 --> 00:02:01.966
Picture two customers who bought the very same green tent.

00:02:01.966 --> 00:02:04.851
One parcel is already on a truck.

00:02:04.851 --> 00:02:08.148
The other tent is still waiting at the warehouse.

00:02:08.148 --> 00:02:12.491
The supplier's delay affects only one of those customers.

00:02:12.491 --> 00:02:16.151
A search for green tents alone would write to both.

00:02:16.151 --> 00:02:21.367
A search for every unshipped order would include customers waiting for boots.

00:02:21.367 --> 00:02:26.819
Keep the intersection, the orders that satisfy both conditions at once.

00:02:26.819 --> 00:02:33.860
Open the remaining draft and check the name, item and wording against the order and supplier notice.

00:02:33.860 --> 00:02:38.929
The useful unit of work was a checked draft for one affected customer.

00:02:38.929 --> 00:02:42.994
Counting messages sent would reward the wrong thing.

00:02:42.994 --> 00:02:48.704
Leo, who works at the shop, checks the proposed recipients against the order list.

00:02:48.704 --> 00:02:53.740
He checks the wording against the notice, then sends the approved messages.

00:02:53.740 --> 00:03:04.250
That is a practical first use: a frequent job, facts the program can find, and a person who can catch a mistake before customers see it.

00:03:04.250 --> 00:03:08.295
One last lesson from real projects: start narrow.

00:03:08.295 --> 00:03:12.148
Scout's first job is order status, and nothing else.

00:03:12.148 --> 00:03:17.964
Once that works, it learns to answer policy questions about returns and shipping.

00:03:17.964 --> 00:03:23.404
Then it handles exchanges, checking the stock before it offers a new size.

00:03:23.404 --> 00:03:28.654
Only later does it touch refunds, and big ones always wait for a person.

00:03:28.654 --> 00:03:36.268
Each new job is added only when the last one works reliably, and that is the path this course follows.
